nodecode-flightlog is a rewrite of piyushpradhan's piyushpradhan/flightlog:

  https://github.com/piyushpradhan/flightlog
  commit c201d602219f6890c380954c1229692e6bfc3f44
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Piyush Pradhan (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the recorder and the judgement on top of it. Every turn
is recorded as it happens -- each tool call with its arguments and duration,
each model round with its tokens, the answer, and how the turn ended -- and
each finished turn is judged by the original's rules, in the original's
words: a claim no check proved, a failure the answer swallowed, a call
repeated in a loop, a job that cost far more than its baseline, one that did
far less work, and one that answered without touching a tool. A job is
compared with its own last healthy runs, the operator reads the verdict in
/flightlog and in the flightlog_runs tool, a flagged run is raised as a
notice, and an acknowledgement silences a rule for that job. The store is one
JSON document under the home instead of a SQLite database.

What was left out, and why: the original hooked ten Hermes callbacks; a
Nodecode turn publishes durable facts on one :frame point, so one hook
records the same ten things and the mapping is in cell.lisp. The subagent
rule is gone, because a spawned turn's success is not a fact the recorder is
handed and inventing one would be worse than missing it. The opt-in tripwire
(a pre-call approval for a looping tool) is gone: Nodecode's approval gate is
another cell's, and the loop is reported here after the fact instead. The
model-written incident report is now the built-in template -- the finding's
sentence and why it matters -- so nothing is dialled; the original's
`hermes flightlog ...` subcommands are /flightlog verbs, and its desktop
page and web dashboard are the command, the tool and the store file.
LICENSE is the original repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
